\f0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb320\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs34 \cf0 Where Neutrinos Come From and Why They Are So Cool 
\f0\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf0 A star implodes in a cataclysmic bust of energy, a scientist measures the small energy discrepancies of radioactive decay, the sun constantly baths us with warmth and showers us with particles, all around us the universe is saturated with the remnants of the Big Bang, and in all of these things there is one thing in common: neutrinos. Although they are hard to detect, these little particles can tell us about everything from the birth of the universe to the nuclear reactions that power our cities. 
\f0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs26 \cf0 Supernova
\f0\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf0 On February 23 1987 detectors deep underground that where designed to detect proton decay suddenly detected a huge number of neutrinos (8 in 5 seconds). Scientist where perplexed by this influx in neutrinos at first until on February 24 a grad student named Ian Shelton announced his observation of a supernova in the Large Magellanic Cloud. This was a core collapse supernova. When the core of a massive star collapses, it crushes the protons and electrons together and neutrinos form.
\f0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\qc\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs22 \cf0 p + e 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  n + \uc0\u957 . 
\f0\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf0 The neutrinos pass straight through the collapsing star before the explosion takes place. This is why the neutrinos where detected before the supernova was visibly observed. When the neutrinos leave, they also take energy away from the star and the star continues to collapse and rebounds out in an explosion that can outshine the brightness of the entire galaxy. Neutrinos are very important to the study of supernovas because they provide an early warning signal and allow scientists to be looking in the right direction before the supernova even takes place.
\f0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs26 \cf0 The Sun
\f0\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf0 Neutrinos are also created in the nuclear reactions that power the core of stars like our sun. Neutrinos are formed in the proton- proton chain. 
\f0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\qc\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs22 \cf0 p + p 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  deuteron + positron + neutrino, 
\f0\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf0 where the deuteron is the nucleus of deuterium. In the sun, 4 hydrogens are being fused into Helium by means of the proton-proton chain. Neutrinos are important because they allow scientists to peek into the interior of the sun and learn about the processes there. All other information about the sun is from electromagnetic radiation that has to pass through the many layers of the sun interacting and changing along the way before traveling through space to us. This whole process can take up 10\super 5\nosupersub  to 10\super 6\nosupersub  years. However, the neutrinos pass cleanly through the sun in a few seconds without interacting and take a mere 8 minutes to travel from the core where they are created to us. 
\f0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs26 \cf0 The Big Bang
\f0\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf0 The greatest source of neutrinos happened some 15 billion years ago. The neutrino was first created 10\super -4\nosupersub  seconds after the big bang. Then at only 1 second after the big bang the universe became transparent to the neutrino allowing them to travel freely through space. At this time the universe had a temperature of about 3*10\super 10\nosupersub . Since the time of the big bang the universe expanded and cooled and continues to expand to this day. There are about 330 million of these neutrinos per m\super 3\nosupersub ; however, these neutrinos have very low energy. They form a cosmic background radiation that is only 2.73 degrees Kelvin today. By studying these neutrinos scientists are able to learn about the universe when it was forming.
\f0\fs24 \

