\f1\b\fs34 \cf0 The Future of Neutrino Research
\f0\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf0 Scientists are constantly coming up with new and ingenious ways to study neutrinos from space. Neutrino telescopes like Super-Kamiokande in Japan use huge vats of water to detect neutrinos (see Figure 3).
\f0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf0 {\*\shppict{\pict {\*\nisusfilename superK}\picw439\pich310\picwgoal6585\pichgoal4650\pngblip }}\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs22 \cf0 Fig. 3 The Super-Kamiokande neutrino telescope (Casper,Dave)
\f0\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf0 The inside of the tank is lined with 11,146 photo-multiplier tubes that detect Cherenkov light. Cherenkov light is emitted by the neutrinos as they pass through the water at near the speed of light.Therefore the detector detects the effect of the neutrinos interacting with the water and not the neutrinos themselves. Telescopes like the Super-Kamiokande are deep underground in order to avoid detecting other particles from cosmic rays. The Homestake telescope uses Chlorine 37 coming from Argon and the GALLEX telescope uses Germanium 71 coming from Gallium as the medium to detect neutrinos instead of water. 
\f0\fs24 \

\f2\fs22 Currently, scientists are building a better neutrino telescope by using the clear polar ice as a medium by which to detect the neutrinos. IceCube is a one-cubic-kilometer new neutrino telescope being built in the South Pole (see Figure 4). It will be an array of 80 strings, each string having 60 optical moduals that are desigened to detect the cherenkov light from emitted from muons, which are a byproduct of the neutrinos interacting with the ice. Scientists are building this new telescope under the South Pole because it allows them to make it incredibly large, to have a very stable place for the detectors, to keep a stable temperature, and to built it deep enough to avoid interference from cosmic rays. 
\f0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf0 {\*\shppict{\pict {\*\nisusfilename IceCube-telescope}\picw225\pich169\picwgoal3375\pichgoal2535\pngblip }}\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs22 \cf0 Fig. 4 The IceCube neutrino telescope (IceCube) 
\f0\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs22 \cf0 The arctic ice also makes a good medium because it is pure, transparent, and free from radioactivity. Scientists hope that by building IceCube they will be able to learn more about far away neutrino sources like gamma ray bursts, supernova, black holes and maybe even dark matter.
\f0\fs24 \

